Female JP4645 traveled to the communal roosting area at night during the egg-laying stage, but remained on the nesting territory during the incubation stage (Figure A9). The incubating female LF1234 also remained on the putative nesting territory during the night (Figure A9), suggesting that the use of the communal roost is contingent upon the nesting status. (https://doi.org/10.1002/ece3.8768); 
"If they survived the dry season, males generally retained territories in the same general area of the island each year, with borders shifting as other birds died or moved.  ...G. fortis and scandens territories overlap broadly, with little indication from field observations of interspecific territoriality (Fig. 6). However, scandens did displace any Geospiza (except the larger G. magnirostris) encountered in their territory, particularly at Opuntia feeding sites or near their nests. As a result fortis territories rarely included the space around scandens nests, whereas scandens territories often included the space around fortis nests (Fig. 6). The use of the territory can be quite thorough. Outside the breeding season, fortis foraged extensively off their territories, while scandens remained on or near their territories much more of the time... A female that changed mates between clutches in 1978 often re-paired with a male having a territory close to that of her first mate. Occasionally young from the first brood flew into the female's new territory and were fed by her; they often begged from the female's new mate, but on all occasions these males ignored them. Attempts by the original male to enter the neighbor's territory were inevitably repelled...  After the young fledged they remained on the territory of the first female's new mate, but were ignored by him... G. fortis is a small, opportunistic breeder, with a large clutch size, an occasionally large but variable population size, a broad diet, and a seasonally varying pattern of territory occupation.  " (https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1095-8312.1984.tb01679.x) 
"The responses by black and partially black fortis territory owners differed significantly. Black males, experienced or not, chased every fortis male intruder, whereas partially black males chased only one of 17 intruders." (Grant 1990 https://doi.org/10.1093/beheco/1.2.161)
